Last week, 7 strangers offered to &quot;grow my LinkedIn.&quot; One even offered to become me.

Below is the underground menu of LinkedIn services landing in my inbox. What they cost you, the DMs they use to sell them, and how I replied

I&apos;ve been banned from LinkedIn twice. Once for having an emoji in my name field. Once for having my birthday on January 8th (their mistake, they apologised). Yet there&apos;s an entire revenue channel operating from the Mines of Moria. 

1. Discounted Sales Navigator seats  
Shared logins. Reseller abuse. Sometimes paid with stolen cards. The seat gets revoked, and your account gets flagged with it. (Yes, that&apos;s him in the picture.)

&quot;Hi Richard, hope you&apos;re doing well! We offer Sales Navigator Core and Advanced at up to 60% below official pricing. Instant activation, 100% safe, 500+ satisfied clients. Shall I share our packages?&quot;

Me: &quot;Does the 60% discount include the account ban, or is that extra?&quot;

2. Fake profiles doing outreach &quot;on your behalf&quot;  
Your brand, attached to a person who doesn&apos;t exist. Prospects notice. They screenshot. They share.

&quot;Hi Richard, we run done-for-you outreach through our network of senior, verified profiles. They build the relationship, you only show up for booked calls. Open to a quick chat this week?&quot;

Me: &quot;So your SDRs are imaginary friends. I stopped having those when I was seven.&quot;

3. Pods and engagement factories  
Fast, fake engagement from people outside your market. LinkedIn detects the pattern. Your reach to actual buyers quietly dies.

&quot;Hey Richard. We&apos;re an invite-only community of 350+ B2B founders supporting each other&apos;s content in the golden hour. Members see 5 to 10x more reach. Only 3 spots left this month.&quot;

Me: &quot;A group chat that claps on command? My family WhatsApp group does that for free.&quot;

4. AI commenting tools  
Generic comments at scale. Easy to spot, easy to detect, and they tell everyone you didn&apos;t care enough to type.

&quot;Hi Richard, your content deserves more visibility. Our tool writes personalised, human-like comments on your ICP&apos;s posts, 50-100 per day, fully on autopilot. Want a free trial?&quot;

Me: &quot;Great insights! Thanks for sharing! 🚀 (Sorry, my AI answered.)&quot;

5. Profile rental  
You &quot;lease&quot; your account to an agency. They run outreach from it. You lose control. When it gets banned, it&apos;s your name on it. Permanently.

&quot;Hi Richard, you can earn $150 to $300 weekly passive income from your LinkedIn account. We only use it for B2B lead generation for US clients. Weekly payments via PayPal. Interested?&quot;

Me: &quot;Sorry, it&apos;s currently occupied. By me. Since 2005.&quot;


Every service on this list sells the same promise: results without the work.
And every one of them uses the same collateral: the account you spent years building.
If a service needs your password, you&apos;re the product.

What&apos;s the most creepy &quot;growth&quot; offer that ever landed in your DMs?

PS: After I reported the Sales Navigator guy, LinkedIn finally agreed with me. They banned his account.
